==============================================================================
N1  arithmetic facts
==============================================================================
  1089 = 33^2 = 3^2 * 11^2                                True
  33 = 2^5 + 1                                            True
  3^5 = 2*11^2 + 1                                        True
  3^9 = 18*1089 + 81                                      True
  2^10 = 1089 - 65 (= 32^2 = 33^2 - 65)                   True
  3^7 - 2^11 = 139 = 2^7 + 11                             True
  3^10 = 1 mod 121  (11 is a base-3 Wieferich prime)      True
  3^9 - 2^10 = 18659 = 47 * 397                           True
  rho = 2^10/3^9 mod 11 = 3, mod 121 = 47 (coincidence: 47 also divides 3^9 - 2^10); multiplicative order mod 121 = 55
  rho^5 mod 121 = 34 = 2^50 mod 121 = 34 (because 3^45 = 1 mod 121)
  3^5 = 2*11^2 + 1 is the last solution of 3^n = 2y^2 + 1 (n = 1, 2, 5): classical.
  None of these involves the prime 2 beyond 2^5 + 1 = 33; HYP-9127 is a statement about 2-adic digits.

==============================================================================
N2  11-adic view: terms rho^(k^3) are 11-adic units; the series does not converge in Q_11
==============================================================================
  k -> rho^(k^3) mod 121 is periodic with period 55; the partial sums S_K mod 121 drift by 0 per period
  so S_K mod 121 is periodic with period 55 and has no 11-adic limit
  (drift 0 is generic: cubing permutes Z/55, so a period sums to (rho^55 - 1)/(rho - 1) = 0 mod 121)
  (|rho^(k^3)|_11 = 1). Nothing 11-adic constrains the 2-adic number X or a putative rational value a/b.

==============================================================================
